module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.DirectSum.Decomposition
public import Mathlib.Algebra.GradedMulAction
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Associator
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.Basic
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# Gradings on twists of the regular module over a graded ring

Let `S` be a commutative ring with a nonnegative grading `𝒮 : ℕ → σ`. For an integer `n`
the twist `S(n)` is the ring `S` with the integer grading `i ↦ S_{i+n}`, where a negative
index is read as the zero subgroup. Likewise a graded module `M` has a twist `M(n)` with
degree `i` piece `M_{i+n}`, and a tensor product of graded modules carries the grading
generated by the pure tensors of homogeneous elements whose degrees add up.

This file proves that the two canonical maps out of such a tensor product are degree
preserving:

* the multiplication `S(a) ⊗_S S(b) → S(a+b)`, which is the left unitor of the tensor
  product, and
* the right unitor `M ⊗_S S(a) → M(a)`.

These are the two facts needed to build the canonical morphisms `𝒪(a) ⊗ 𝒪(b) → 𝒪(a+b)` and
`M̃ ⊗ 𝒪(a) → M(a)̃` of sheaves on `Proj(S)` out of the functoriality of the associated-sheaf
construction, which is how Tag 01MU sets up the twisting construction.

## Implementation notes

Nothing here carries a stacks attribute for Tag 01MU, deliberately. That tag's own assertions are
about the invertible locus of a twist and the comparison map for associated modules; the two
degree-preservation statements proved here are the unlabelled inputs that the construction of
those maps consumes. Tagging them would report the tag as formalized when its assertions are
not, so the attribute is withheld until the sheaf-level statements are proved. Those need the
associated sheaf of a graded module on `Proj` (Tag 01M6) and its functoriality (Tags 01M7,
01MK), none of which exist in this library yet.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.GradedModule.homogeneousSubmodule`: a homogeneous piece of an
  arbitrary `SetLike` grading, read as a `ℤ`-submodule of the underlying additive group.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.integerGrading`: a nonnegative grading extended by
  zero to all integer degrees.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.twistGrading`: the grading of `S(n)`.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.shiftedGrading`: the grading of `M(n)`.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.tensorGrading`: the grading of a tensor product of
  two graded modules.

## Main results

* `Stacks.GradedModule.map_mem_of_mem_tensorGrading`: a linear map out of a
  tensor product is degree preserving as soon as it is degree preserving on pure tensors of
  homogeneous elements.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.mul_mem_integerGrading`: the integer-extended grading
  of a graded ring is multiplicative.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.lid_mem_twistGrading`: multiplication
  `S(a) ⊗_S S(b) → S(a+b)` is degree preserving.
* `Stacks.GradedModule.rid_mem_shiftedGrading`: the right unitor
  `M ⊗_S S(a) → M(a)` is degree preserving.

## Implementation notes

The grading families are taken in the `SetLike`/`AddSubgroupClass` generality in which Tag
01MU states these assertions, so that the two results apply verbatim to the twist gradings
built from an arbitrary `GradedRing` representation. Only `AddSubgroupClass` is used of the
target family, which is why `map_mem_of_mem_tensorGrading` needs no decomposition
hypothesis: degree preservation is a statement about the additive subgroup generated by the
homogeneous pure tensors, not about the decomposition it induces.

The scalar action of `ℕ`-degrees on `ℤ`-degrees is the `VAdd ℕ ℤ` instance `n +ᵥ i = n + i`,
declared locally because Mathlib has no such instance; it is the one with respect to which
`SetLike.GradedSMul 𝒮 𝒨` expresses that a graded ring acts on an integer-graded module.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01MU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01MU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01MN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01MN)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0AG2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AG2)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.GradedModule

open scoped TensorProduct

universe u v v' v'' w t t' t''

/-- The degree-`i` piece of an arbitrary additive-subgroup grading, read as a `ℤ`-submodule
of the underlying additive group. -/
public def homogeneousSubmodule {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S]
    [AddSubgroupClass σ S] (𝒮 : ℕ → σ) (i : ℕ) : Submodule ℤ S where
  carrier := 𝒮 i
  zero_mem' := zero_mem (𝒮 i)
  add_mem' := fun ha hb => add_mem ha hb
  smul_mem' := fun n _ ha => zsmul_mem ha n

/-- Membership in `homogeneousSubmodule 𝒮 i` is membership in `𝒮 i`. -/
@[simp]
public theorem mem_homogeneousSubmodule {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S]
    [AddSubgroupClass σ S] (𝒮 : ℕ → σ) (i : ℕ) (x : S) :
    x ∈ homogeneousSubmodule 𝒮 i ↔ x ∈ 𝒮 i := Iff.rfl

/-- A nonnegative grading, extended by the zero submodule to all integer degrees. -/
public def integerGrading {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S]
    [AddSubgroupClass σ S] (𝒮 : ℕ → σ) (i : ℤ) : Submodule ℤ S :=
  if 0 ≤ i then homogeneousSubmodule 𝒮 i.toNat else ⊥

/-- The degree-`i` piece of the twist `S(n)` is `S_{i+n}`. -/
public abbrev twistGrading {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S]
    [AddSubgroupClass σ S] (𝒮 : ℕ → σ) (n : ℤ) : ℤ → Submodule ℤ S :=
  fun i => integerGrading 𝒮 (i + n)

/-- The grading of the shifted module `M(n)`, with degree-`i` piece `M_{i+n}`. -/
@[sga "6 VI 1.2"]
public abbrev shiftedGrading {M : Type v} {τ : Type t} [SetLike τ M] (𝒨 : ℤ → τ) (n : ℤ) :
    ℤ → τ :=
  fun i => 𝒨 (i + n)

/-- The degree-`k` piece of a tensor product of graded modules is generated by the pure
tensors of homogeneous elements whose degrees add to `k`. -/
public def tensorGrading {S : Type u} {M : Type v} {N : Type v'} [CommRing S]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module S N]
    {τ : Type t} [SetLike τ M] [AddSubgroupClass τ M]
    {υ : Type t'} [SetLike υ N] [AddSubgroupClass υ N]
    (𝒨 : ℤ → τ) (𝒩 : ℤ → υ) (k : ℤ) : AddSubgroup (M ⊗[S] N) :=
  AddSubgroup.closure {z | ∃ i j : ℤ, i + j = k ∧
    ∃ m : M, m ∈ 𝒨 i ∧ ∃ n : N, n ∈ 𝒩 j ∧ z = m ⊗ₜ[S] n}

/-- The pure tensor of homogeneous elements of degrees `i` and `j` lies in the degree-`k` piece
of the tensor grading when `i + j = k`. -/
public theorem tmul_mem_tensorGrading {S : Type u} {M : Type v} {N : Type v'} [CommRing S]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module S N]
    {τ : Type t} [SetLike τ M] [AddSubgroupClass τ M]
    {υ : Type t'} [SetLike υ N] [AddSubgroupClass υ N]
    (𝒨 : ℤ → τ) (𝒩 : ℤ → υ) {i j k : ℤ} (hij : i + j = k)
    {m : M} (hm : m ∈ 𝒨 i) {n : N} (hn : n ∈ 𝒩 j) :
    m ⊗ₜ[S] n ∈ tensorGrading 𝒨 𝒩 k :=
  AddSubgroup.subset_closure ⟨i, j, hij, m, hm, n, hn, rfl⟩

/-- A linear map out of a tensor product of graded modules takes the degree-`k` piece of the
tensor grading into a given additive subgroup as soon as it does so on the pure tensors of
homogeneous elements whose degrees add to `k`. -/
public theorem map_mem_of_mem_tensorGrading {S : Type u} {M : Type v} {N : Type v'}
    {P : Type v''} [CommRing S]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module S N]
    [AddCommGroup P] [Module S P]
    {τ : Type t} [SetLike τ M] [AddSubgroupClass τ M]
    {υ : Type t'} [SetLike υ N] [AddSubgroupClass υ N]
    {ω : Type t''} [SetLike ω P] [AddSubgroupClass ω P]
    (𝒨 : ℤ → τ) (𝒩 : ℤ → υ) (Q : ω) (f : M ⊗[S] N →ₗ[S] P) (k : ℤ)
    (hf : ∀ i j : ℤ, i + j = k → ∀ m : M, m ∈ 𝒨 i → ∀ n : N, n ∈ 𝒩 j → f (m ⊗ₜ[S] n) ∈ Q)
    {z : M ⊗[S] N} (hz : z ∈ tensorGrading 𝒨 𝒩 k) :
    f z ∈ Q := by
  induction hz using AddSubgroup.closure_induction with
  | mem w hw =>
      obtain ⟨i, j, hij, m, hm, n, hn, rfl⟩ := hw
      exact hf i j hij m hm n hn
  | zero => simpa only [map_zero] using zero_mem Q
  | add x y _ _ ihx ihy => simpa only [map_add] using add_mem ihx ihy
  | neg x _ ih => simpa only [map_neg] using neg_mem ih

section Mul

variable {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S] [AddSubgroupClass σ S]
  (𝒮 : ℕ → σ)

/-- In a nonnegative degree, the integer-extended grading is the original homogeneous piece. -/
@[simp]
public theorem integerGrading_of_nonneg {i : ℤ} (hi : 0 ≤ i) :
    integerGrading 𝒮 i = homogeneousSubmodule 𝒮 i.toNat := by
  simp only [integerGrading, hi, ite_true]

/-- In a negative degree, the integer-extended grading is the zero submodule. -/
@[simp]
public theorem integerGrading_of_neg {i : ℤ} (hi : i < 0) : integerGrading 𝒮 i = ⊥ := by
  simp only [integerGrading, Int.not_le.mpr hi, ite_false]

/-- An element of a negative-degree piece of the integer-extended grading is zero. -/
public theorem eq_zero_of_mem_integerGrading_of_neg {i : ℤ} (hi : i < 0) {x : S}
    (hx : x ∈ integerGrading 𝒮 i) : x = 0 := by
  rw [integerGrading_of_neg 𝒮 hi] at hx
  simpa using hx

/-- An element of a nonnegative-degree piece of the integer-extended grading is homogeneous
of that degree for the original grading. -/
public theorem mem_homogeneous_of_mem_integerGrading {i : ℤ} (hi : 0 ≤ i) {x : S}
    (hx : x ∈ integerGrading 𝒮 i) : x ∈ 𝒮 i.toNat := by
  rw [integerGrading_of_nonneg 𝒮 hi] at hx
  simpa using hx

/-- An element of the integer-extended grading is zero or lies in a nonnegative degree, where it
is homogeneous for the original grading. -/
private theorem eq_zero_or_mem_of_mem_integerGrading {i : ℤ} {x : S}
    (hx : x ∈ integerGrading 𝒮 i) : x = 0 ∨ 0 ≤ i ∧ x ∈ 𝒮 i.toNat := by
  rcases lt_or_ge i 0 with hi | hi
  · exact .inl (eq_zero_of_mem_integerGrading_of_neg 𝒮 hi hx)
  · exact .inr ⟨hi, mem_homogeneous_of_mem_integerGrading 𝒮 hi hx⟩

/-- The integer-extended grading of a graded ring is multiplicative. -/
public theorem mul_mem_integerGrading [GradedRing 𝒮] {i j : ℤ} {x y : S}
    (hx : x ∈ integerGrading 𝒮 i) (hy : y ∈ integerGrading 𝒮 j) :
    x * y ∈ integerGrading 𝒮 (i + j) := by
  obtain rfl | ⟨hi, hx'⟩ := eq_zero_or_mem_of_mem_integerGrading 𝒮 hx
  · simp
  obtain rfl | ⟨hj, hy'⟩ := eq_zero_or_mem_of_mem_integerGrading 𝒮 hy
  · simp
  have hij : 0 ≤ i + j := by omega
  rw [integerGrading_of_nonneg 𝒮 hij, mem_homogeneousSubmodule,
    show (i + j).toNat = i.toNat + j.toNat from by omega]
  exact SetLike.mul_mem_graded hx' hy'

end Mul

section Twists

/-- The degrees of a graded ring act on the degrees of an integer-graded module by addition.
Mathlib has no `VAdd ℕ ℤ` instance; this is the one with respect to which
`SetLike.GradedSMul 𝒮 𝒨` says that a nonnegatively graded ring acts on an integer-graded
module, and it is the instance Tag 01MU and Tag 0AG2 use. -/
local instance instVAddNatInt : VAdd ℕ ℤ where
  vadd n z := (n : ℤ) + z

/-- Multiplication takes the graded tensor product of two twists of the regular module into
the twist by the sum of the indices. This is the degree-preservation statement behind the
canonical map `𝒪(a) ⊗ 𝒪(b) → 𝒪(a+b)` on `Proj(S)`. -/
public theorem lid_mem_twistGrading {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S]
    [AddSubgroupClass σ S] (𝒮 : ℕ → σ) [GradedRing 𝒮] (a b i : ℤ) (z : S ⊗[S] S)
    (hz : z ∈ tensorGrading (twistGrading (S := S) 𝒮 a) (twistGrading (S := S) 𝒮 b) i) :
    (TensorProduct.lid S S).toLinearMap z ∈ twistGrading (S := S) 𝒮 (a + b) i := by
  refine map_mem_of_mem_tensorGrading _ _ _ _ i (fun p q hpq m hm n hn => ?_) hz
  have h := mul_mem_integerGrading 𝒮 hm hn
  rw [show p + a + (q + b) = i + (a + b) from by omega] at h
  simpa only [LinearEquiv.coe_coe, TensorProduct.lid_tmul, smul_eq_mul] using h

/-- The right unitor takes the graded tensor product of `M` and the `a`-th twist of the
regular module into the shifted grading of `M(a)`. This is the degree-preservation statement
behind the canonical map `M̃ ⊗ 𝒪(a) → M(a)̃` on `Proj(S)`. -/
public theorem rid_mem_shiftedGrading {S : Type u} {σ : Type w} [CommRing S] [SetLike σ S]
    [AddSubgroupClass σ S] (𝒮 : ℕ → σ) [GradedRing 𝒮]
    {M : Type u} [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M]
    {τ : Type t} [SetLike τ M] [AddSubgroupClass τ M]
    (𝒨 : ℤ → τ) [DirectSum.Decomposition 𝒨] [SetLike.GradedSMul 𝒮 𝒨]
    (a i : ℤ) (z : M ⊗[S] S)
    (hz : z ∈ tensorGrading 𝒨 (twistGrading (S := S) 𝒮 a) i) :
    (TensorProduct.rid S M).toLinearMap z ∈ shiftedGrading 𝒨 a i := by
  refine map_mem_of_mem_tensorGrading _ _ _ _ i (fun p q hpq m hm n hn => ?_) hz
  rw [LinearEquiv.coe_coe, TensorProduct.rid_tmul]
  obtain rfl | ⟨hq, hn'⟩ := eq_zero_or_mem_of_mem_integerGrading 𝒮 hn
  · simp
  have h := SetLike.GradedSMul.smul_mem (A := 𝒮) (B := 𝒨) hn' hm
  rw [show (q + a).toNat +ᵥ p = i + a from by
    change ((q + a).toNat : ℤ) + p = i + a
    omega] at h
  exact h

end Twists

end Stacks.GradedModule

end
